package com.hpcai270

// Days 186–202: Ceph, distributed object/block/file storage and data platforms.
val hpcDaysPart17 = listOf(
Day(186,"Ceph architecture deep dive","MON, OSD, MGR and clients","Ceph is a distributed storage platform with object, block and filesystem interfaces. Learn the cluster roles before learning commands.","ARCHITECTURE\n\nClient → RADOS → OSDs. MON maintains cluster maps/quorum; OSDs store data; MGR provides management/telemetry; gateways and CephFS add service interfaces.","KEY IDEA\n\nData placement is algorithmic. The cluster does not depend on one central storage controller for all I/O.","FAILURE\n\nOSD failure changes cluster state and can trigger recovery/backfill. MON quorum and network health affect control-plane availability.","LAB\n\nDraw a 5-node Ceph cluster with MONs, OSDs and a client. Mark data and control paths.","INTERVIEW\n\nCompare a centralized enterprise array with Ceph. Discuss distributed control, scale-out, operational complexity and failure behavior.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand Ceph as a distributed system, not a RAID replacement."),
Day(187,"Ceph CRUSH","Deterministic data placement","CRUSH maps objects to placement groups and OSDs using cluster topology and rules. It avoids a central lookup table for every object.","MODEL\n\nObject → hash → placement group → CRUSH rule → OSD set. Topology-aware rules can avoid correlated failures.","WHY\n\nPlacement can adapt when nodes/OSDs change, but movement creates recovery traffic. Data distribution quality affects performance and capacity balance.","LAB\n\nExplain how adding an OSD can cause data movement. Draw a rack-aware rule that avoids putting all replicas in one rack.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is CRUSH important for scale-out storage? It makes placement distributed and topology-aware.","GOAL\n\nThink about data placement mathematically and physically."),
Day(188,"Ceph pools and placement groups","Logical organization of data","Pools define policy boundaries such as replication, erasure coding and application use. Placement groups distribute objects within pools.","MODEL\n\nApplication → pool → PG → OSD set. Pool configuration affects protection, performance and recovery.","OPERATIONS\n\nToo few or too many PGs can create imbalance or overhead depending on version and scale. Use current vendor guidance rather than memorized constants.","LAB\n\nDesign pools for block, object and filesystem workloads. State protection and failure-domain assumptions.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy should different workloads sometimes use different pools? Because protection, performance and lifecycle requirements differ.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand logical storage policy before capacity math."),
Day(189,"Ceph replication versus erasure coding","Capacity versus performance trade-offs","Replication is simple and often fast for active data. Erasure coding improves capacity efficiency but can introduce more complex write/recovery behavior.","DESIGN\n\nHot random-write workloads often prefer replicated pools; large sequential/object data may suit EC depending on implementation and workload.","RECOVERY\n\nEC recovery reconstructs missing fragments and can consume significant CPU/network bandwidth.","LAB\n\nCalculate raw-to-usable capacity for 3× replication and 8+3 EC. Then list operational trade-offs.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is 3× replication not automatically ‘better’ than EC? It trades capacity for simpler/faster protection.","GOAL\n\nMake protection decisions from workload and failure model."),
Day(190,"Ceph recovery and backfill","The cluster after failure","Recovery restores redundancy; backfill moves data to satisfy placement rules. Both consume resources and can interfere with user workloads.","FLOW\n\nFailure → degraded PGs → recovery/backfill → healthy placement → rebalance.","CONTROL\n\nRecovery tuning must balance data safety and application performance. Aggressive recovery shortens exposure but can create severe contention.","MONITOR\n\nPG states, recovery rate, client latency, network utilization, OSD utilization and remaining capacity.","LAB\n\nWrite an incident plan for one OSD failure in a busy cluster. Define when to throttle and when not to.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is recovery traffic part of normal capacity planning? Because every production system spends time in degraded/rebalancing states.","GOAL\n\nDesign for the failure state."),
Day(191,"Ceph RBD","Distributed block devices","RBD provides virtual block devices backed by Ceph. It is useful for VMs, containers and applications requiring block semantics.","PATH\n\nGuest/application → RBD client → librbd/kernel RBD → RADOS → OSDs.","PERFORMANCE\n\nObject size, queue depth, network, client CPU and OSD latency affect performance. RBD is not the same as a local NVMe disk.","AI\n\nRBD can support control-plane/VM workloads around AI infrastructure, but high-throughput training data may use a different data path.","LAB\n\nCompare RBD conceptually with NVMe-oF and local NVMe for latency and shared-access semantics.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhen would you choose RBD over CephFS or RGW? Answer from application semantics.","GOAL\n\nSelect the right Ceph interface."),
Day(192,"CephFS","Distributed POSIX filesystem","CephFS provides a POSIX filesystem on top of Ceph. Metadata and data services have distinct responsibilities.","PATH\n\nClient → CephFS → metadata service + RADOS data objects → OSDs.","PERFORMANCE\n\nMetadata-heavy and bulk-data workloads can stress different components. Placement and client concurrency matter.","HPC\n\nCephFS can be useful for general shared files, but a dedicated HPC parallel filesystem may be preferred for specific extreme-scale workloads.","LAB\n\nDraw CephFS versus Lustre data paths and list where metadata lives.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is the architectural difference between CephFS and Lustre even though both expose POSIX files?", "GOAL\n\nCompare distributed filesystem designs."),
Day(193,"Ceph RGW and S3","Object access through Ceph","RGW exposes S3-compatible object APIs on Ceph. It allows object workloads to use distributed storage without exposing filesystem semantics.","PATH\n\nS3 client → RGW → Ceph object namespace → RADOS → OSDs.","USE CASES\n\nArtifacts, datasets, backups, logs and archives. Performance depends on object size, concurrency and gateway capacity as well as backend OSDs.","LAB\n\nDesign a 10-node RGW service with multiple gateways and explain why the gateways themselves can become a bottleneck.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy does adding OSDs not necessarily double S3 throughput? Gateway CPU/network, object distribution and client concurrency can dominate.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand object service architecture."),
Day(194,"Ceph monitoring","From cluster health to root cause","Ceph health summaries are starting points. Senior engineers correlate health state with OSD latency, PG state, network and client performance.","EVIDENCE\n\nCluster health → MON quorum → OSD up/in → PG state → recovery → disk latency → network → client latency.","LAB\n\nCreate a dashboard vocabulary and map each panel to an action. Separate symptoms from root-cause evidence.","INTERVIEW\n\nIf Ceph says HEALTH_WARN, what do you do? Identify the exact warning, determine impact and inspect the affected subsystem before clearing the alert.","GOAL\n\nTreat health output as a clue, not a diagnosis."),
Day(195,"Distributed consistency","CAP, quorum and practical storage","Distributed storage must balance consistency, availability and partition behavior. Do not reduce this to slogans; understand what the actual service promises.","MODEL\n\nNetwork partition can prevent nodes from agreeing. Quorum mechanisms protect against split-brain in control planes.","STORAGE\n\nData consistency semantics vary by interface. POSIX, object and block services expose different application contracts.","LAB\n\nExplain how a control-plane quorum failure differs from an OSD/data-path failure.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat does ‘split brain’ mean in distributed storage? Give a concrete risk and the mechanism used to prevent conflicting authoritative state.","GOAL\n\nReason about distributed systems instead of treating them as giant arrays."),
Day(196,"Distributed storage networking","The network is the backplane","Ceph traffic includes client I/O, replication/recovery and cluster communication. Network saturation can therefore affect both data and control paths.","TRAFFIC\n\nClient → OSD, OSD ↔ OSD, MON/MGR control, recovery/backfill.\n\nSeparate or prioritize traffic when architecture requires it.","FAILURE\n\nPacket loss or latency can look like storage slowness. Correlate network telemetry with OSD and client latency.","LAB\n\nDraw traffic classes for a 25/100/400G Ceph network and identify possible contention.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can storage recovery make network problems worse? Recovery itself generates traffic and may compete with client I/O.","GOAL\n\nOperate distributed storage as a networked system."),
Day(197,"Distributed storage capacity planning","Raw, usable and operational capacity","Capacity planning must include protection overhead, reserved space, failure headroom, rebuild requirements and growth.","MODEL\n\nRaw → protection overhead → filesystem/object overhead → reserved operational space → usable target.\n\nDo not run a distributed cluster near 100% full.","FAILURE\n\nLow free space can make recovery impossible or dramatically slower. Capacity is therefore part of resilience.","LAB\n\nCreate a 1 PB raw cluster plan with 3× replication and 20% operational headroom. Calculate approximate usable capacity.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy does usable capacity fall further during failure/rebuild scenarios? Protection and placement constraints require spare capacity.","GOAL\n\nSize for normal and degraded operation."),
Day(198,"Ceph versus enterprise arrays","Different operational philosophies","Traditional arrays centralize much control; Ceph distributes it across software and nodes. Both can be excellent when matched to the problem.","COMPARE\n\nArray: integrated hardware/software, mature management, predictable support model. Ceph: commodity/server hardware, software-defined, scale-out and flexible interfaces.","AI\n\nCeph can serve platform and object/block workloads around AI environments, while specialized parallel systems may target extreme training throughput.","LAB\n\nWrite a decision matrix for enterprise NAS, high-performance parallel filesystem and Ceph. Include operations and staffing, not just performance.","INTERVIEW\n\nWould you replace every enterprise array with Ceph? Explain why architecture is workload-specific.","GOAL\n\nAvoid technology tribalism."),
Day(199,"Storage platform lifecycle","Build, operate, upgrade, retire","A senior engineer owns lifecycle, not just installation. Track versions, support status, capacity, spares, contracts, security and end-of-life.","LIFECYCLE\n\nDesign → procurement → deployment → validation → operations → upgrade → scale → decommission → data disposition.","AI\n\nAI infrastructure changes quickly. A storage platform must survive software and GPU generation changes without becoming an isolated legacy island.","LAB\n\nCreate a lifecycle checklist for a 5-year storage platform, including annual capacity/performance review.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat would make you retire a storage platform before hardware end-of-life? Support risk, performance gap, security, operational cost or inability to meet workload requirements.","GOAL\n\nThink beyond day-one architecture."),
Day(200,"Storage architecture checkpoint","Design a distributed platform","Combine Ceph concepts with your enterprise storage background.","WHITEBOARD\n\nClients → network → distributed storage nodes → protection → media. Add management, monitoring, identity and backup.","QUESTIONS\n\nCRUSH? PG? OSD? MON quorum? RBD? CephFS? RGW? replication? EC? recovery? failure domain? capacity headroom?",
"SCENARIO\n\nOne rack loses power. Explain what survives, what degrades, how recovery starts and how you protect application performance.","LAB\n\nDesign a 500 TB multi-tenant Ceph cluster and produce capacity, network, protection and recovery assumptions.","INTERVIEW\n\nDefend why Ceph is or is not appropriate for three workloads: VM disks, S3 datasets and 500-GPU training scratch.","STANDARD\n\nYou can now compare distributed storage with parallel filesystems and enterprise arrays using first principles.")
)
